Diagnostic (off by default): trace knote copyin to catch the #15 data.ptr aliasing at its source.

Passes 46-52 established that shellspawn's two knotes (EVFILT_READ on the launcher socket, EVFILT_PROC
NOTE_EXIT on the shell) end up registered in one epoll with the SAME data.ptr, which misroutes the shell's
NOTE_EXIT and hangs teardown. On Linux libkqueue never free()s a knote until the whole kqueue closes
(kq_tofree drained only in kqueue_cleanup), and knote_new is a plain calloc, so two live knotes cannot
share an address -- yet they do, deterministically (the guest has no ASLR). To resolve the contradiction
without guesswork, log, guarded by CIDER_TRACE_KNOTE, the (filter, ident, lookup result) for every change
and the (filter, ident, knote, dupfd) for every fresh create. This is diagnostic only; the real fix lands
once the trace shows whether knote_new returns a live address or a knote is reused across filters.
---
--- a/src/common/kevent.c
+++ b/src/common/kevent.c
@@ -129,6 +129,11 @@
 
     kn = knote_lookup(filt, src->ident);
     dbg_printf("knote_lookup: ident %d == %p", (int)src->ident, kn);
+    /* #15 diagnostic: two epoll registrations shared one data.ptr; log which knote each change resolves
+     * to (lookup hit = reuse, NULL = fresh) to catch aliasing at its source. Off unless env is set. */
+    if (getenv("CIDER_TRACE_KNOTE"))
+        fprintf(stderr, "CIDER_KNOTE copyin filt=%d ident=%ld flags=0x%x lookup=%p\n",
+                (int) src->filter, (long) src->ident, (unsigned) src->flags, (void *) kn);
     if (kn == NULL) {
         if (src->flags & EV_ADD) {
             if ((kn = knote_new()) == NULL) {
@@ -154,6 +159,9 @@
             } 
             knote_insert(filt, kn);
             dbg_printf("created kevent %s", kevent_dump(src));
+            if (getenv("CIDER_TRACE_KNOTE"))
